# Project Overview

RCC Column ML predicts RCC column **load–deformation** response (peak capacity and backbone curves) from design parameters, with interactive graphs in Streamlit. Organization mirrors the Kaneo/jira monorepo pattern adapted for Python/ML.

## Project Structure

```
RCC_Column/
├── apps/
│   ├── web/              # Streamlit UI (pages + components)
│   └── docs/             # Living documentation (canonical domain docs)
├── src/
│   ├── dataset/          # Build / audit ML tables
│   ├── train/            # Peak & curve training
│   ├── predict/          # Inference helpers
│   └── shared/           # Constants, schemas, IO utils
├── data/
│   ├── raw/              # Properties CSV + PEER downloads
│   └── processed/        # dataset_ml.csv, curves_backbone.csv
├── models/               # Saved joblib pipelines
├── outputs/              # Metrics CSVs, static plots
├── tests/                # Mirror src feature folders
├── plans/                # Numbered design / experiment plans
├── scripts/              # One-off CLI helpers
├── .cursor/rules/        # Cursor agent rules
├── requirements.txt
├── pyproject.toml        # Ruff / pytest
└── README.md
```

## Technology Stack

| Layer | Choice |
|-------|--------|
| Language | Python 3.10+ |
| Data | pandas, numpy |
| Models | scikit-learn (RF, MLP), XGBoost |
| Persist | joblib |
| Explain | SHAP |
| Charts | Plotly (UI), matplotlib (static PNGs) |
| UI | Streamlit under `apps/web/` |
| Lint/format | Ruff (`pyproject.toml`) |

**Out of v1:** React frontend, LSTM, Optuna deep tuning, ACI equation benchmark.

## Key Principles

1. **Feature folders** — each domain owns its code under `src/{feature}/`
2. **Thin UI** — Streamlit pages call `src/` services; no training logic in `apps/web/`
3. **Same schema** — PEER and ANSYS share identical columns + `source` tag
4. **No leakage** — always split by `specimen` / `specimen_id`
5. **Docs + rules stay in sync** — platform guide Changelog updated with every feature

## Development Workflow

See `setup.md`. Short version:

```bash
python -m venv .venv && .venv\Scripts\activate   # Windows
pip install -r requirements.txt
npm install

npm run build:data
npm run train
npm run dev          # Streamlit → http://localhost:8501
```

